.TH FLEET-TRACE 1 "2026-11-08" "cti-agent 1.6" "CTI Fleet Manual"
.SH NAME
fleet-trace \- keep and read a record of what each heartbeat did
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fleet\-trace show
.RI [ FILE " | " \fB\-\-last\fR ]
.RB [ \-\-full ]
.br
.B fleet\-trace list
.RB [ \-n
.IR N ]
.br
.B fleet\-trace prune \-\-days
.I N
.br
.B fleet\-trace init
.br
.B fleet\-trace tee \-\-out
.I FILE
.B \-\-plain
.I FILE
.RB [ \-\-max\-mb
.IR N ]
.PP
.B sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace
.I subcommand
.SH DESCRIPTION
Every orchestrator heartbeat that
.BR run\-checkin (1)
starts is recorded as it happens: the model and tools it started with, each
tool it called and with what, whether the call succeeded or was denied, what
it said, and at the end how many turns it took, the tokens it used and what it
cost. The record is the
.B claude
CLI's own
.B stream\-json
output, one JSON object per line, kept in
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/traces .
.PP
Before traces, the only account of a beat was whatever the model chose to
print. A trace answers "why did the heartbeat escalate on Tuesday?" from what
it actually did.
.PP
A trace holds everything the beat read, including untrusted advisory and mail
text. It is handled like a report: mode 0600 in a 0700 directory, never
mailed, never committed. The beat itself cannot read it:
.B run\-checkin
grants no
.B Read
on
.I state/
and does not grant
.BR fleet\-trace .
.PP
.B show
and
.B list
replace every control character, ANSI escape and bidirectional override with
.B ?
before printing, so text the beat read cannot rewrite what the operator's
terminal shows.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BR show " [\fIFILE\fR | " \-\-last "] [" \-\-full ]
Print one beat as a timeline. With no argument, or
.BR \-\-last ,
the newest trace. A
.I FILE
without a slash is looked for in the trace directory. Tool results are shown
as a size and ok or ERROR; with
.B \-\-full
their text is printed as well, sanitized, up to 4000 characters each.
.TP
.BR list " [" \-n " \fIN\fR]"
One line per beat, newest first: the trace's name and size, ok or ERROR,
turns, tool calls denied, and cost. Default 10; 0 lists all. A trace with no
result line (the beat was killed) shows
.BR none .
.TP
.BI "prune \-\-days " N
Delete traces older than
.I N
days. Only files named
.BI checkin\- YYYYMMDD T HHMMSS .jsonl
in the trace directory are touched, never through a link.
.B run\-checkin
runs it after every beat with
.BR CHECKIN_TRACE_DAYS .
.TP
.B init
Create the trace directory, mode 0700, and prove a file can be written in
it. Prints the directory.
.B run\-checkin
runs it before each beat and runs the beat untraced if it fails.
.TP
.BI "tee \-\-out " FILE " \-\-plain " FILE " \fR[\fP\-\-max\-mb " N\fR]\fP
Used by
.BR run\-checkin ,
not by people. Reads the beat's output on standard input and does three
things with it: keeps every line in the new file
.B \-\-out
(created 0600, refused if it exists), prints the timeline to standard output
for the journal, and writes to
.B \-\-plain
only what the CLI's text mode used to print: lines that are not JSON
(standard error), API error messages, and the final result.
.B cti\-budget record
classifies that file, by words such as
.BR 429 ,
.B quota
and
.BR billing ;
the timeline's token counts or the tool output in front of it would turn
ordinary beats into holds.
.IP
The trace stops at
.B \-\-max\-mb
megabytes (default 20) with a
.B truncated
marker; the reading does not. A trace that cannot be written stops the
trace, not the reading, so the beat is never cut off by a full disk.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B FLEET_HOME
The state directory; traces are in
.IR $FLEET_HOME/state/traces .
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /var/lib/cti\-agent/state/traces/checkin\-*.jsonl
One file per beat. The first line is
.BR {"type":"fleet_trace",...} ;
a line the CLI printed that was not JSON is kept as
.BR {"type":"fleet_trace_text","text":...} ;
every other line is the CLI's own event.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 1
.BR show :
no trace, or it cannot be read.
.BR tee :
the trace or the plain file could not be written in full; the reading still
went to the end.
.BR init :
the directory cannot be made or written.
.TP
.B 2
Usage: an unknown subcommand or argument.
.SH EXAMPLES
The last beat:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace show \-\-last
.EE
.PP
The last day of beats at a glance:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace list \-n 48
.EE
.PP
One beat with every tool result:
.PP
.EX
sudo cti\-agent fleet\-trace show checkin\-20261108T093000.jsonl \-\-full | less
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR run\-checkin (1),
.BR cti\-budget (1),
.BR fleet\-journal (1),
.BR cti\-agent (1)
.PP
HARNESS.md, H1.
